# 13:30 What We've Learned

We began with this question.

> **How do computers represent and prepare text before AI can learn from it?**

We now have the answer.

Computers first identify individual characters.

Unicode code points provide that identity.

Next, text is represented as bytes so it can be stored or transmitted.

UTF-8 is one standard that performs that job.

Finally, text may be cleaned and standardized before later AI processing.

That preparation can include normalization, whitespace cleanup, removing empty lines, or many other project-specific steps.

These are different jobs.

```text
Character
        ↓
Unicode code point
        ↓
Character identity

Text
        ↓
UTF-8
        ↓
Storage or transmission

Source text
        ↓
Preparation steps
        ↓
Prepared text
```

Notice what we have **not** done.

We have not learned anything from the text.

We have not extracted meaning.

We have not trained an AI model.

We've simply prepared text so later stages can work with it.

Those later stages are what the rest of this course is about.

In the next lesson, we'll build on today's foundations by asking another important question.

If every character has a unique identity, how can we build a dependable collection of all the characters our AI model is allowed to use?